The major problem in your strategy is returning to levels you've
already gone past. NetHack is a turn-based, single-player game, not
some sort of endless MMORPG. It doesn't pay to hang around and collect
items. Your best bet is to go through levels, check for loot, then
leave. Don't back-track any more than you have to; the grass is always
greener deeper down in the dungeon.

Why shouldn't you backtrack? Mainly, you'll get really bored, and
annoyed at the game. It also increases the chances that you'll be
killed. There's usually no real good reason for backtracking.

Have a stash up on dlvl 4?
     Everything you need, you should have with you, in your main
inventory, or stuffed into your bag of holding. If you can't take it
with you without burdening yourself, you either don't need it or
there's something else that you're carrying that you don't need.
Especially drop things like potions (except forms of potions of
healing), spellbooks (but always take identify, polymorph, magic
missile, and finger of death with you), excess weapons (if it's not
your primary mode of combat, ditch it), and excess wands (dump: useless
and empty wands. keep: 1 wand of striking, 1 wand of SSD for the
sanctum).

Waiting for some monster to drop a good item?
     You'll find better items later on in the dungeon. Those luxury
items you desire are not very helpful in the early game, and are only
useful for assurance (better equipment so you won't die) in the late
game. The castle wand will grant you at the very least four items, even
if you recharge a (0:1) wand with its own charge. Generally, nothing
above the castle will grant you the better, wished-for items(dragon
scale mail and quest artifacts), so there's no point in hanging around
for them.

Trying to level up?
     It's better to just clear a level and move on. You'll get enough
experience to get you to the castle, and after that you won't be
gaining levels through experience, anyway. Leveling up usually doesn't
help you too much compared to the benefits of more items deeper down,
and it increases the chance of you being killed.

Trying to sacrifice for some good items?
     Unless you've already spent tens of thousands of turns extincting
or pudding farming, your god probably won't grant you anything but
artifact weapons. While these are very useful, you do not need more
than one. Having too many will also reduce the chance of a successful
quest-artifact wish, and having one of those is far better than
bragging about having Trollsbane, Ogresmasher, and Orcrist in your
Minetown stash.



